.PP
Steps - starting with the processing of interior faces:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
The creation of \fBInterFace\fP objects in the interior and the necessary duplication of nodes occur simultaneously\&.
.IP "2." 4
Interface objects are constructed from the second range, also duplicating nodes at the model- or domain boundaries\&. No new nodes are inserted if the boundary terminates inside of a higher dimensional region\&. In this case, interior and exterior perimeter nodes on the \fBSplitBoundary\fP perimeter are assigned the same, pre-existing perimeter node inherited from the converted boundary\&.
.PP

.PP
During the construction of all \fBInterFace\fP objects, the higher dimensional neighbors are assigned and the corresponding \fBElement\fP faces are remembered\&.

.PP
.IP "3." 4
Former higher-dimensional \fBElement\fP neighbors that are now separated by the \fBSplitBoundary\fP are disconnected from one another assigning their neighbor pointers to 'nullptr'\&. (this step is not necessary if the whole connectivty is rebuilt anyway)
.IP "4." 4
The new \fBInterFace\fP objects are connected with one-another so that a \fBSplitBoundary\fP constructor has all the necessary information to distinguish interior from perimeter\&.
.IP "5." 4
Finally the node-to-parent element connectivity of the \fBInterFace\fP nodes needs to be rebuilt restricting parent element access to the side of the interface that the node forms part of
.PP

